---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/firepush-alternative';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'alternatives/firepush-alternative');
const desc = description(site, 'alternatives/firepush-alternative');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Firepush still offer SMS and email campaigns?",
    "answer": "Its November 18, 2026 product-scope notices say it is now a web push app and no longer offers SMS or email campaigns. Legacy help and listing text should not be treated as current channel availability."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can a push subscriber CSV activate another sender?",
    "answer": "Do not assume it can. Firepush\u2019s export guidance says push subscriber export is unavailable and subscriptions use browser-specific tokens. Obtain a supported migration plan and verify real subscription eligibility before sending."
  },
  {
    "question": "Must we replace working Firepush push campaigns?",
    "answer": "No. Keeping push while replacing legacy email or SMS is a valid option. Assign each channel an owner and reconcile overlapping recovery queues and purchase exits."
  },
  {
    "question": "What does the discount example show?",
    "answer": "At $634 revenue and 40% gross margin, fixed goods cost is $380.40. A 15% discount leaves $538.90 revenue and $158.50 gross profit instead of $253.60, losing $95.10 before other costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-16 container">
<a href="/alternatives" class="link">← All alternatives</a>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-10">
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Firepush alternatives: separate legacy channels from push continuity</h1>
<p>Start by deciding whether you are replacing a legacy SMS/email workflow, the current push sender, or both. Those jobs have different data, permission and cutover requirements.</p>
<h2>Use the explicit current product scope</h2>
<p>Checked November 25, 2026. Firepush’s <a href="https://getfirepush.com/blog/sms-campaigns/">SMS article</a> and <a href="https://getfirepush.com/blog/targeted-email-marketing/">email article</a>, updated November 18, 2026, explicitly state that the product now focuses on web push and no longer offers SMS or email campaigns. These scope notices take precedence for this decision over stale indexed help or listing copy describing an all-channel app. This is a channel change, not evidence that the company has shut down.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://getfirepush.com/">current product site</a> describes push recovery, welcome, price-drop and back-in-stock workflows. Retaining working push is a useful option when only the old email/SMS arrangement needs replacement. Evaluate whether the store needs another push provider at all before introducing subscription continuity work.</p>
<h2>Keep three separate inventories</h2>
<p>For legacy email, retain available addresses, permission evidence, unsubscribes, bounce exclusions, templates and historical campaign records. For legacy SMS, retain available numbers, purpose-specific consent evidence, STOP records, sender details and pending campaign records. Ask support what historical data remains accessible; an old help article describing an export menu is not a guarantee that the menu still exists for your account.</p>
<p>For push, document the store origin, service worker configuration, subscription identifiers available to the operator, permission state and active automations. Browser permission is not an email address or SMS permission. Do not merge an anonymous browser subscription with a customer solely because both viewed the same cart.</p>
<h2>Resolve token continuity before uninstalling</h2>
<p>Firepush’s <a href="https://getfirepush.com/help/subscribers/export-subscribers/">export guidance</a> says push export is unavailable and tokens are browser-specific. Its <a href="https://getfirepush.com/help/getting-started/switching-push-provider/">switching guide</a> describes provider/service-worker constraints. Treat those as reasons to obtain a supported, store-specific plan from both providers, not a promise of automatic token transfer. Their legacy SMS instructions do not override the newer channel-scope notices.</p>
<p>Record whether the proposed destination requires visitors to subscribe again, which browser/origin combinations it supports, and who changes the service worker. In a controlled browser profile, exercise allowed, denied and revoked permissions, an existing subscription and a fresh visitor. Keep prompt ownership with one app during the transition. Acceptance evidence should show which sender can reach which subscription and how revocation stops future attempts; do not infer continuity from an imported row count.</p>
<h2>Evaluate replacements by channel</h2>
<p>If retaining Firepush push, evaluate an email/SMS replacement separately. <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/klaviyo-email-marketing">Klaviyo’s listing</a> describes segmentation and automated Shopify messaging; <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/omnisend">Omnisend’s listing</a> describes forms and prebuilt ecommerce workflows. Those are useful trial candidates for the legacy-channel job, not evidence that either can receive Firepush push tokens. Have the candidate demonstrate the required event and exclusion cases using permitted test contacts, then compare operating effort and channel costs. For a push replacement, require a specific browser/subscription migration demonstration before procurement.</p>
<h2>Reconcile every queued reminder</h2>
<p>These are proposed acceptance exercises, not observed tests. Snapshot active push automations and any retained legacy schedule records, with cart ID, channel, enrollment time and send state. Choose one recovery owner per cart/channel and document how a purchase cancels pending work across the retained and replacement senders. Cancel or drain old queues deliberately before enabling new enrollment; do not replay an entire historical event feed.</p>
<p>Place an order while a reminder is waiting, then replay the abandonment event and deliver an order update late. The operator should find one completed cart and no new recovery send. Test a push revocation during the wait separately from an email unsubscribe or SMS STOP. Each should update the relevant exclusion without inventing permission for another channel. Where shared identity is unavailable, avoid promising perfect cross-channel deduplication and choose a conservative enrollment rule.</p>
<h2>Make the notification useful when the cart changes</h2>
<p>For an out-of-stock variant, withhold the stale recovery push or use an available-item path that the merchant has approved. Back-in-stock alerts and cart recovery should not create competing discount offers. Change a price after enrollment and verify notification text and landing-cart totals; an expired notification must not advertise yesterday’s deal.</p>
<p>Test a mobile click into the correct cart and an expired cart link into a useful fallback. A gift-size objection needs sizing help; a minimum quantity needs an accurate requirement; shipping shock needs region-specific delivery information. Failed financing should return to an available payment choice, with support intervention pausing promotional reminders where appropriate. For existing customers, reconcile welcome, win-back and cart enrollment before adding another push.</p>
<h2>Protect margin at the final checkout</h2>
<p>The retained $634 basket at 40% gross margin has fixed goods cost $380.40 and gross profit $253.60. A 15% discount removes $95.10 revenue: $538.90 remains, with $158.50 gross profit. This excludes shipping, fees, returns and messaging costs. Exercise a bundle, automatic discount and recovery code together and verify the actual final basket before approving notification copy. A discount does not repair an inventory or checkout defect, and attributed revenue does not prove incremental profit.</p>
<h2>Keep a recoverable cutover record</h2>
<p>Save the subscription decision, permission tests, canceled queue entries, purchase-exit evidence and operator contacts. If the new sender cannot establish eligibility or suppress a purchased cart, pause it. Restore only a retained, verified old workflow with current exclusions; rollback does not recreate revoked subscriptions or make discarded tokens transferable. Delay uninstalling the retained push app until the approved continuity plan and queue reconciliation are complete. Do not restore legacy SMS/email campaigns based on old documentation.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="FAQ: leave Firepush?" />
</section>
</BaseLayout>
